 more people will follow her  footsteps and make their voices heard  India is home to an estimated 400  million people who can't read or write  the rate of illiteracy is especially  high among women many of whom can barely  write their names thirteen year old  Satya Khan wants to do much more than  that  Satya Khan has already been in class the  entire day but she's still eager for  more hardly anything can stop this  thirteen-year-old from pursuing her love  of learning everyone's hope I'd love to  come to school even if I'm tired if I  get a fever if I'm also sick so that  time also come to school the first time  she went to school was seven years ago  in a village just outside India's  capital New Delhi she got there thanks  to literacy India an organization that  helps underprivileged children who would  otherwise not be in school the  organization focuses particularly on  young girls their education czar often  neglected in rural communities out of  safety concerns or because parents need  them to help run the household and  although India passed the Right to  Education Act in 2009 challenges still  remain definitely right to education  Hill and he has brought some changes in  the terms of more enrollment more  attendance more facilities into the  school but in terms of the changing the  minds of the communities it's not  effective so to change the mindset of  the committee we'll have to go on the  ground it will have to reach them you'll  have to convince them so I think it will  take time  Sadia represents of what can happen when  these kinds of challenges are overcome  she lives walking distance from the  school and shares a room with her  parents and sister the family doesn't  have a lot of money and neither parent  received much of an education for her  mother Raveena school was never an  option growing up but today she feels  her lack of education limits what she  can do and wants a different life for  her daughter's this I feel very sad that  I'm not educated I've only had just a  little bit of schooling  but now when I want to use the phone I  need to wait for my children or my  husband to help and there are lots of  things like that that I have to think  about  Sonya's father azim dyes clothes for a  living but business is slow right now  the machines he usually works with are  barely in use Azim doesn't have the  qualifications to change professions  easily so he sees sadhyas education as a  potential opportunity for the entire  family that if she studies and make  something of herself then it will be to  our benefit our life will change and so  will hers they will definitely be  changed and it will be good the signs  are promising Satya already uses what  she's learned to help her father with  things he used to struggle with like  taking orders or writing bills and  sadhyas recent admission to a more  established school is also a major step  one she hopes will help her achieve her  dream  it's been two years since Monier  al-sheikh fled Syria like hundreds of  thousands of other refugees he struggled  through the Balkans before finally  reaching his goal Germany DW has been  taking a look at how he's faring Monier  al-sheikh is the newest member of the  volunteer fire brigade in the western  town of Augsburg once a week the fire  fighters gather to simulate a rescue  operation it's challenging but familiar  taking part in these exercises is  important  this is devastation as a refugee it's  very important to give something back  these people have opened their doors to  us and we have to show them that we're  grateful it's our duty one of the  biggest hurdles for Munir is  understanding the orders  slowly but surely he's beginning to find  his feet in the beginning it's difficult  to wrap your head around everything but  he's doing really well  and it's lonely getting to grips with  the specialist vocabulary we use here  when he's not at the fire station monia  volunteers in the local community  helping with odd jobs like hedge  trimming after arriving in Germany he  founded the initiative new neighbors to  help migrants integrate he became a  point of contact for new arrivals  helping other migrants with bureaucracy  and coming to terms with life in Germany  despite his volunteer work monir is  struggling himself he was only recently  able to find his own apartment and  despite his best efforts is still  searching for a paid job for now he has  to make do with the 300 euros he  receives from the government each month  many are often thinks back to two years  ago then he was travelling to Germany  along the so called balcan route but got  stranded in angery the situation in  Hungary was unbelievable we slept on the  ground for four days said that if the  situation doesn't improve I'll just have  to go back to Syria  [Music]  eventually the border opened and one  year was taken to Germany by bus along  with thousands of others now he's trying  to make a new home here he's applied for  nearly 50 jobs across the country  every time this application has been  rejected life in Germany he says is  harder than he ever would have imagined  [Music]  we share was until recently one of the  people smuggling hubs of Africa Bashir  AMA was involved in this illegal  business in the desert city of Agadez  but since the government started  cracking down on illegal migration  people smugglers have had to find other  work Bashir amah  has a lot of time on his hands to talk  shop with the club soccer trainer Bashir  used to make a lot of money by helping  migrants across the Sahara Desert all  the way to Libya but when the government  cracked down on people smugglers Bashir  decided to call it quits he now helps  fund the city's biggest soccer club this  is the club of people smugglers I'm a  people smuggler the Vice President is a  people smuggler and so is everyone who's  in charge here and so with the investors  his experience as a smuggler comes in  handy for Bashir's latest business deal  eight of his players are migrants who  had been stranded in Agadez where Bashir  recruited them for his team player 66  from Ivory Coast also once dreamed of  becoming a soccer superstar in Europe  instead Muhammadu now plays for  Basheer's club efforts this season  wasn't going so well but next season  it's going to be better but sure now  tries to prevent them from making the  trek to Europe mostly because it's in  his own interest they are his top  players after all but also because more  and more ruthless gangs are taking over  new shares trafficking business the  roots are checked everywhere at the  moment that's why the traffickers take  different routes through the desert and  they're very dangerous  Bashir takes us to one of the hidden  desert cities just outside of Anka des  where many of the new gangs are  operating from it's said to be ruled by  the Mafia this visit is not without risk  for the former people smuggler relations  with the new guys are understandably  strained but he says he's well-connected  so they won't harm him he's mostly  interested in scouting Talent  among the hundreds of stranded migrants  who ran out of money to continue their  travels many have been stuck here for  months but nothing seems to deter them  from their journey my final destination  is Europe no matter what obstacles I run  into I'm not afraid I'm only gonna die  once in the end Bashir manages to find a  potential soccer player from Ivory Coast  and invites him to come to tryouts  it's where he will have to prove he has  enough talent to play for New Year 2nd  division and most importantly whether he  is ready to give up his dream of Europe  for now  [Music]  you

